$ enola log --help
exit: 2
--- stdout
--- stderr
Usage: enola log [flags] [repo_path|config_path]

Show what this repository's architecture has done over time: one line per
snapshot enola recorded, with what changed since the one before it.

Read-only. It reports what was observed and never snapshots to fill a gap.

Oldest first, which is the opposite of `git log`: a changelog answers "what
landed recently", and this answers "how did it get like this", which runs
forward. With --graph, lines therefore diverge downward at a branch and
converge downward at a merge.

With --backfill it instead BUILDS that timeline from the repository's own
commit history, snapshotting past commits so a repo enola has never seen still
has a past to read. It only reads the repository — trees are extracted to a
temp dir — and re-running resumes rather than repeating.

EXPERIMENTAL. Every snapshot is recorded as a revision; turn that off with
`history:` / `enabled: false` in your config.

Flags:
  -all
    	include working revisions (uncommitted trees)
  -backfill
    	build the timeline from the repository's own commit history
  -dry-run
    	with --backfill, list what would be snapshotted and stop
  -graph
    	draw the branch topology beside the revisions
  -json
    	emit the entries as JSON
  -n int
    	show at most this many revisions (0 for all) (default 20)
  -oneline
    	one line per revision (default true)
  -sample string
    	which commits to backfill: all, merges, tags or daily (default "all")
  -since string
    	only revisions taken after this time (RFC3339, or a duration like 72h)
  -stat
    	break each revision's delta down by fact kind
